{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1788509200106,"sessionID":"ses_f949057d0ffeQN24RvdTqDk317","part":{"id":"prt_06b74753d0017JxMrL2CoiurhL","messageID":"msg_06b746c00001KDk9rZdm7hXGlL","sessionID":"ses_f949057d0ffeQN24RvdTqDk317","type":"reasoning","text":"The patch applied. Now, note one behavioral detail: in `_compile`, when reading the stateful pages marker from a previous backend build, the app previously called `self._enable_state()` after re-compiling stateful pages. The patch removed that — but the re-compiled stateful pages may create state classes during evaluation (tracked via `_stateful_pages`), and... hmm, wait. If `enable_state=True`, the app already enabled state in `__post_init__` (`_state = State` and `_setup_state()`). If `enable_state=False`, we should NOT enable state even if stateful pages exist — that matches the requirement \"handle the case where state is explicitly disabled but some pages might appear to use state features - the framework should not attempt to enable state\".\n\nBut wait — if `enable_state=False` and a page creates state classes during evaluation (`_stateful_pages[route] = None`), the page will still compile. The compiled page would reference state events in the frontend (e.g., `handle_frontend_exception` via ErrorBoundary with stateful app wraps...). Actually with `enable_state=False`, `self._state` stays None → app wraps get `stateful=False` → `on_error=noop()` and no overlay. And `_compile_contexts`... let me check: does `_compile` still compile contexts when state is None? Let me look at the current `_compile` in the testbed to see how contexts are compiled.","time":{"start":1788509189437,"end":1788509200099},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The patch applied. Now, note one behavioral detail: in `_compile`, when reading the stateful pages marker from a previous backend build, the app previously called `self._enable_state()` after re-compiling stateful pages. The patch removed that — but the re-compiled stateful pages may create state classes during evaluation (tracked via `_stateful_pages`), and... hmm, wait. If `enable_state=True`, the app already enabled state in `__post_init__` (`_state = State` and `_setup_state()`). If `enable_state=False`, we should NOT enable state even if stateful pages exist — that matches the requirement \"handle the case where state is explicitly disabled but some pages might appear to use state features - the framework should not attempt to enable state\".\n\nBut wait — if `enable_state=False` and a page creates state classes during evaluation (`_stateful_pages[route] = None`), the page will still compile. The compiled page would reference state events in the frontend (e.g., `handle_frontend_exception` via ErrorBoundary with stateful app wraps...). Actually with `enable_state=False`, `self._state` stays None → app wraps get `stateful=False` → `on_error=noop()` and no overlay. And `_compile_contexts`... let me check: does `_compile` still compile contexts when state is None? Let me look at the current `_compile` in the testbed to see how contexts are compiled.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}}}
